Now I understand the vulnerability. Let me analyze `lex_multiline_string`:

```c
static void *lex_multiline_string(struct lexer *lexer)
{
    char *end = (peek(lexer) == '"') ? "\"\"\"" : "'''";

    advance_n(lexer, strlen("'''") - 1);

    do {
        if (!strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)) {
            emit(lexer, LEXEME_STRING);
            lexer->pos += 3;

            return lex_config;
        }
    } while (next(lexer) != '\0');

    return lex_error(lexer, "EOF while scanning multiline string");
}
```

The issue: When we enter `lex_multiline_string`, we've already consumed the first quote character (e.g., `'`). Then `advance_n(lexer, strlen("'''") - 1)` advances by 2 more characters. So after this, `lexer->pos` points to the character after the 3 quotes.

Then in the loop, `strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)` reads 3 bytes from `lexer->pos`. If the input is exactly `'''` (3 quotes) with nothing after, then `lexer->pos` points to the end of the buffer (or the null terminator). Reading 3 bytes from there could read past the buffer - a global-buffer-overflow READ.

Let me look at how the lexer is set up, particularly `next`, `peek`, `advance_n`, and the buffer structure.

